    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Vienna began as the Roman camp of Vindobona in the 1st century, became the seat of the Babenberg dukes in 1155 and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1278, who made it the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperors</a>.</li><li>It withstood Ottoman attacks in 1529 and 1683, hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1814 and 1815, and in the later 19th century replaced its fortifications with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ringstrasse">Ringstrasse</a> as it grew to more than two million people.</li><li>After the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in 1918 it was the capital of a republic, was annexed by Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1938, and was occupied by the four Allied powers until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-state-treaty">Austrian State Treaty</a> of 1955.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When King Richard the Lionheart was captured near Vienna in 1192, his ransom of 50,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> marks, about 10 to 12 tons of silver, paid for a mint and the city walls built around the year 1200.</p></aside>
    </section>

