    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 97</span><span class="rf-text">Construction of the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> camp of Vindobona begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1155</span><span class="rf-text">Henry II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> makes Vienna his capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1221</span><span class="rf-text">Vienna receives the rights of a city and a staple right</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1529</span><span class="rf-text">The first Ottoman siege of Vienna fails</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1683</span><span class="rf-text">An army led by John III Sobieski defeats the Ottoman army besieging the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1814</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> meets to redraw the political map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1938</span><span class="rf-text">Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> annexes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and Hitler speaks at the Heldenplatz</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1955</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-state-treaty">Austrian State Treaty</a> ends the four-power occupation</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius.jpg" alt="Marcus Aurelius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MSR-ra-61-b-1-DM.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daniel Martin, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Vienna lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at the eastern edge of the Vienna Woods, the north-eastern foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, where they give way to the Pannonian Basin. The area was inhabited by modern humans from the Upper Palaeolithic, and the name Vindobona, which comes from a Celtic language, suggests that people lived there before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> came. The earliest settlement of the inner city lay south of the meandering Danube.</p><p>In the 1st century the Romans set up the military camp of Vindobona in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, on the site of today's city centre, with an adjoining civilian town to secure the borders of the empire. Construction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> camp began around 97 CE, and it was occupied by Legio X Gemina. At its peak Vindobona had a population of around 15,000 and formed part of a trade and communications network across the empire. It was raised to the status of a municipium in 212. Even today the streets of the First District show where the camp placed its walls and moats. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> may have died there in 180 during a campaign against the Marcomanni.</p><p>The German name Wien comes from the river Wien, not from Vindobona, and first appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> Annals, which note a battle at a place named Wenia in 881.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Vienna&#8221; cites where it covers Etymology and exonyms, Middle Ages and early modern period:</p><ul><li>John Philip Spielman, The city &amp; the crown: Vienna and the imperial court, 1600–1740 (Purdue University Press, 1993)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna.jpg" alt="University of Vienna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_the_University_of_Vienna.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original: Universität Wien Vector: Lệ Xuân, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Vienna was an important site of trade as early as the 11th century. In the Exchange of Mautern between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> and Margrave Leopold IV it is mentioned as a Civitas for the first time, which points to a well-ordered settlement. In 1221 the city received the rights of a city and a staple right, which obliged all traders passing through to offer their goods there. The Viennese became middlemen in trade, with far-reaching connections along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> basin and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and Vienna became one of the most important cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> duke Rudolf IV, called the Founder, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a> in 1365 and began the Gothic nave of the Stephansdom. After the Ottoman siege of 1529 Vienna was made into a fortress with eleven bastions, a moat and an open glacis, and these works accounted for much of the building well into the 17th century. After 1683 the city became largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>, with garden palaces such as the Belvedere and Schönbrunn built in the suburbs. It is estimated that 150,000 people lived there in 1724 and 200,000 in 1790. The first factories were built from that time, and after 1837 Vienna was the centre of the railway network.</p><p>In 1858 the fortifications were demolished and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ringstrasse">Ringstrasse</a> was built in their place. Its historicist buildings characterise the architecture of Vienna to this day. The period peaked in the World Exhibition of 1873, just before a stock market crash. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Censuses</a> from 1869 onwards showed a population of 2,031,000 in 1910.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Vienna&#8221; cites where it covers Demographics, Economy:</p><ul><li>Darwin Porter, Frommer's Vienna &amp; the Danube Valley (John Wiley &amp; Sons, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Ottokar II of Bohemia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PecetpoII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Otakarus, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 976 the Margraviate of Ostarrîchi was given to the Babenberg family, and Vienna lay at its border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. In 1155 Margrave Henry II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> made Vienna his capital, and in 1156 Austria became a duchy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/privilegium-minus">Privilegium Minus</a>, with Vienna the seat of all later dukes. Duke Leopold V captured King Richard the Lionheart near Vienna in 1192, and the ransom allowed the creation of a mint and the building of city walls.</p><p>In 1278 Rudolf I took control of the Austrian lands after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a> and began to establish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule. In Vienna this took a long time, because partisans of Ottokar stayed strong and there were several uprisings against Albert I. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> was the imperial residence and Vienna stood in its shadow. Vienna received its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1469. In 1485 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> of Hungary and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-army-of-hungary">Black Army</a> conquered the city, which served as the capital of Hungary until 1490. After the Blood Judgment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiener-neustadt">Wiener Neustadt</a> in 1522 the city stood under direct imperial control, and in 1556 it became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>.</p><p>Vienna became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> in 1804, and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> after the compromise of 1867. In 1918 it became the capital of the Republic of German-Austria, and in 1919 of the First Republic of Austria.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Vienna&#8221; cites where it covers Middle Ages and early modern period:</p><ul><li>John Philip Spielman, The city &amp; the crown: Vienna and the imperial court, 1600–1740 (Purdue University Press, 1993)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner.jpg" alt="Anton Bruckner" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Anton Bruckner</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Anton_Bruckner_(1868).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Vienna became Protestant rather quickly in the 16th century and was then re-Catholicised. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> were brought to the city in 1551 and gained a large influence at court, and Melchior Khlesl, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Vienna from 1600, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were expelled from the city in 1421 and 1422 under Albert V, and in 1670 they were driven out of their ghetto of 50 years, after which Leopoldstadt became a place where many Jews lived. In 1783 Joseph II modernised the city administration and created the Magistrate, and he closed the graveyards within the city.</p><p>Vienna became a leading centre of music, from the age of Viennese Classicism through the early part of the 20th century. Haydn, Mozart and Beethoven worked there, and it was home to Schubert, Brahms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Bruckner</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Mahler</a> and Johann Strauss I and II. The first Viennese café was opened in 1685 by the Armenian businessman Johannes Diodato, and café culture flourished in the early 19th century. Around 1900 Vienna was a centre of the Jugendstil, with Otto Wagner and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-secession">Vienna Secession</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a>, the first psychoanalyst, lived there. Industrialisation and migration from other parts of the empire raised the population sharply, and around 1900 Vienna had the second-largest Czech population in the world after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><p>Karl Lueger was the leading figure of city politics around 1900. He brought fresh water from the mountains by a new pipeline and created a belt of meadows and forests round the city, but his policy was coupled with an intense anti-Semitism, which had popular support.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/anschluss"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anschluss.jpg" alt="Anschluss" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anschluss_Heldenplatz1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Heinrich Hoffmann, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first Ottoman siege came in 1529. The medieval walls barely held until epidemics and an early winter forced the Ottoman army to withdraw. In 1683 the new fortifications let the city hold out for two months, until the Ottoman army was defeated by an army led by the Polish king John III Sobieski. The Turkish Wars turned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after it. In 1679 the Great Plague struck, and nearly a third of the inhabitants are said to have died of bubonic plague.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> the French took the city in 1805 without a battle and in 1809 after heavy fire. The March Revolution of 1848 forced Metternich to resign. In July 1927 a riot broke out after three far-right paramilitary members were acquitted of killing two members of the Republikanischer Schutzbund, and the Palace of Justice was set on fire. Police killed at least 84 protesters, and five policemen also died. In February 1934, after Dollfuss had dissolved parliament and banned the Communist Party, civil war spread to Vienna, where socialists barricaded themselves in housing estates until the army shelled the Karl-Marx-Hof and the Schutzbund surrendered.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a> of March 1938 Viennese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were harassed and plundered, and in November 1938 all synagogues and prayer houses were destroyed except the Stadttempel. Of almost 200,000 Jews in Vienna, around 120,000 were driven to emigrate and approximately 65,000 were killed. From 17 March 1944 there were 51 air raids, and around a third of the city centre was destroyed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> entered the city on 6 April 1945 and took it after several days of fighting.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/red-vienna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-vienna.jpg" alt="Red Vienna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/red-vienna">Red Vienna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl-Marx-Hof_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dreizung, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> met from September 1814 to June 1815 and redrew the political map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the social life around it moved Prince de Ligne to say that the congress dances but does not progress. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ringstrasse">Ringstrasse</a> and its buildings, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> palaces and gardens and the historic centre remain the city's architectural ensemble, and the centre was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2001.</p><p>Between the wars the city's Social Democrats ran the municipality from 1919 to 1934, in the period called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-vienna">Red Vienna</a>. In 1921 Vienna became a state of its own, separate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>. Over 60,000 apartments were built in municipal housing blocks between 1925 and 1934, among them the Karl-Marx-Hof, and these Gemeindebauten still serve as affordable housing. In 1938 the city was enlarged by 91 municipalities to 1,224 km², and the enlargement was reversed in 1954.</p><p>After the war the four powers divided Vienna into sectors from September 1945, with the First District an international zone whose control passed monthly among them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-state-treaty">Austrian State Treaty</a> was signed in May 1955 and came into force on 27 July 1955, and by October all soldiers had left. In the 1970s Vienna became an official seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, and the Vienna International Centre was built. Vienna is today the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and a state in its own right.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Vienna&#8221; cites where it covers Middle Ages and early modern period:</p><ul><li>John Philip Spielman, The city &amp; the crown: Vienna and the imperial court, 1600–1740 (Purdue University Press, 1993)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/ave-maris-stella">Ave maris stella<span>c. 850</span></a><a href="/songs/gaudeamus-igitur">Gaudeamus igitur<span>1781</span></a><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser<span>1797</span></a><a href="/songs/wiegenlied">Wiegenlied (Brahms's Lullaby)<span>1868</span></a><a href="/songs/the-red-army-is-the-strongest">The Red Army Is the Strongest<span>1920</span></a><a href="/songs/lili-marleen">Lili Marleen<span>1939</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Vienna grew from the Roman camp of Vindobona into the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">Babenbergs</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. It withstood Ottoman sieges in 1529 and 1683, hosted the Congress of Vienna, and is today the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</em></p>

